body:
To be honest, both behaviors do not make sense :grinning:.

Agreed, `require("fs")` should not return an empty object. But to test the availability of the `fs`-module I think it's most common to use try-catch:

``` javascript
var fs;

try {
    fs = require("fs");
} catch(err) {
    // fs not available in this environment
}
```

As far as I can tell it's intentional: https://github.com/webpack/webpack/issues/187 (to behave like [browser-resolve](https://github.com/defunctzombie/node-browser-resolve)). And here's webpacks defaults https://github.com/webpack/node-libs-browser . 

You can fix it in your config, for examply by writing your own fs browser module (module.exports = false)

``` js
    resolve: {
      alias:{
          fs: require.resolve('./false.js')
      }
  }
```

Perhaps there's a cleaner way which doesn't change fs module globally though.

And if you wanted it to throw, you could use IgnorePlugin (which supports context parameter).

The question was addressed to @sorka :grinning:.

It's definitely not desirable in all situation so webpack shouldn't do it by default. I was just wondering if it is possible as a plugin. Because there are modules which only need the content of a specific directory:

``` javascript
var fs = require("fs");
var path = require("path");
language = fs.readFileSync(path.resolve(__dirname, "i18n", language));
```

In this situation webpack is able to include all contents of the `i18n`-directory as a so-called context. Of course this is a very specific problem, but it might come in handy when you want to make a module work in the browser where the required files are statically determinable (as in the example above).

btw. you can get back to the old behavior with

``` js
{
  node: {
    fs: "empty"
  }
}
```

http://webpack.github.io/docs/configuration.html#node
